                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "kick-drum":</h3><p>The <b>kick drum</b> or bass drum can be challenging to record because of the instrument&#8217;s high SPL, and the need to capture both the high-end &#8220;click&#8221; of the beater impact as well as the low-end resonant tone of the shell. Large drums move a lot of air, which can damage some microphones.</p><p>Due to these special needs, many mic manufacturers have created dedicated &#8220;kick drum&#8221; microphones, which typically employ moving-coil dynamic elements (which tend to be the most durable transducers), and which typically are pre-equalized to emphasize the HF and LF portions of the frequency spectrum.</p>
